+# Experimental short-tap input-method request
+
+This optional component builds upstream Foot 1.28.0 with the narrow patch in
+this directory. A short tap on the focused terminal grid commits an input-method
+disable followed by a separate enable. This gives Labwc a fresh activation when
+the on-screen keyboard was hidden while the same terminal retained focus.
+Scrolling, dragging, decoration taps and physical mouse handling keep their
+existing paths. Foot's own IME setting and compositor protocol availability
+still apply.
+
+## Build and select explicitly
+
+The default release continues to use the signed Arch Foot package. To build and
+select this experiment in a normal release:
+
+```sh
+ORCHARD_CHECKOUT=/path/to/orchard python tools/build.py \
+  --version YOUR_VERSION --output out/YOUR_VERSION --foot-touch-ime
+```
+
+Or build only the component with the normal Orchard build environment:
+
+```sh
+ORCHARD_CHECKOUT=/path/to/orchard python desktop/build-foot --output out/foot-tools
+```
+
+`--archive FILE` accepts a cached archive only when its SHA256 matches the pin.
+`release.public_root.foot_files(directory)` verifies the resulting manifest,
+AArch64 executable, IME feature, source, patch and host-fixture receipt before
+returning an optional root overlay. Release assembly accepts `--foot-tools DIR`;
+release audit checks the installed binary, corresponding source and metadata.
+Only `usr/bin/foot` replaces a signed package file; the same-version Foot client,
+terminfo and desktop resources remain supplied by Arch. The locally built binary
+is identified by its own receipt and is not described as an Arch-signed binary.
+Rebuilding without the option selects the signed package again.
+
+The producer uses a signed Arch container toolchain, disables dependency
+fallback downloads and runs upstream's configuration test plus the actual-C
+callback fixture. It retains the pristine source archive, patch, MIT license,
+package/compiler versions and fixture result alongside the executable. A build
+container needs an init/reaper (`--init` when provisioning native workers).
+
+## Source and compatibility
+
+- [Official Foot source](https://codeberg.org/dnkl/foot), version 1.28.0,
+  commit `ab33c9a19d626f8d0ac0bd1adfdba21946ada948`.
+- [Official Arch packaging, 1.28.0-2](https://gitlab.archlinux.org/archlinux/packaging/packages/foot/-/blob/1.28.0-2/PKGBUILD)
+  selects that upstream tag. Its Git-source checksum differs from the archive
+  checksum used by this producer.
+- The pristine `input.c` Git blob is
+  `f6ba9dec76ab39fb32a840cc9af54e1b65df3746`, matching the patch preimage.
+  The exact published patch applies with `--fuzz=0`.
+- [Original bring-up patch and evidence](https://github.com/thisiscamk/ipad-air-j72-linux/tree/main/code/desktop)
+  used this change in a combined desktop with keyboard-focus and touch-worker
+  fixes. Repeated hide → tap → type passed there; that result does not isolate
+  this patch from those other changes.
+
+The source pin records both archive and patch hashes. No firmware, calibration,
+credentials or device logs are inputs to this component.
+
+## Qualification boundary
+
+Host tests compile the actual patched `wl_touch_up` and upstream `ime_enable` /
+`ime_disable` functions with protocol mocks. They check ordered commits, 100
+repeated taps in one focused window, scroll/drag/decoration paths, missing
+protocol, disabled IME, wrong touch ID and focus changes. These are protocol
+requests, not a running compositor or keyboard test.
+
+The normal native Omarchy GUI, this producer's binary, and its combination with
+`OMARCHY_KEYBOARD_AUTO=1` still need a device test. Do not make this the default
+until menu, keyboard icon and repeated same-window hide → tap → type pass on the
+normal source-built desktop. A successful source build does not qualify the
+whole J72 platform or its power policy.
